      m_Calls: []
  m_text: False ribs comprise the 8th through 12th pairs of ribs, distinguished by
    their lack of direct anterior attachment to the sternum. Ribs 8 to 10, often
    termed vertebrochondral ribs, connect to the sternum indirectly through the costal
    cartilage of the rib above. Ribs 11 and 12 are also known as floating ribs because
    they have no anterior attachment; their cartilages end in the muscles of the
    abdominal wall. While they provide some protection for the upper abdominal organs,
    the mobility of the false ribs plays a crucial role in breathing and allowing
    flexibility for bending and twisting movements.
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
