  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: The coracoid process is a small, hook-shaped bony projection from the superior
    anterior part of the scapula. Situated just lateral to the scapular notch and
    anterior to the glenoid fossa, this structure serves as a critical anchor point
    for several muscles and ligaments. Notably, the coracobrachialis, pectoralis
    minor, and the short head of the biceps brachii muscles attach here. Additionally,
    the coracoacromial ligament and the coracohumeral ligament find their origins
    on this process. The coracoid process plays a pivotal role in shoulder stability
    and function.
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
