      m_Calls: []
  m_text: 'Description: The pulmonary semilunar valve, often simply called the pulmonary
    valve, is a three-cusp or tri-leaflet valve that controls the flow of blood from
    the right ventricle to the pulmonary artery.


    Location: Positioned at the
    exit of the right ventricle, leading into the main pulmonary artery.


    Function:
    During the contraction phase (systole) of the heart, the valve opens, allowing
    deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to flow into the pulmonary artery
    and subsequently to the lungs for oxygenation. During the relaxation phase (diastole),
    the valve closes to prevent the backflow of blood from the pulmonary artery back
    into the right ventricle.'
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
  m_sharedMaterial: {fileID: 2180264, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
