      m_Calls: []
  m_text: The right femur, often referred to as the thigh bone, is the longest, largest,
    and strongest bone in the human body. It spans from the hip to the knee and articulates
    with the pelvis at the acetabulum, forming the hip joint, and with the tibia
    at the knee joint. The femur has a rounded head, a neck, and a long shaft, with
    distinct trochanters serving as sites for muscle attachment. The femur plays
    a pivotal role in weight-bearing and locomotion. Injuries or disorders, like
    fractures or osteoporosis, can compromise its function and stability, often necessitating
    medical or surgical management.
