  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: The manubrium is the broad, upper segment of the sternum, resembling the
    shape of a shield. Situated superiorly in the center of the thoracic cage, it
    articulates with the body of the sternum at the manubriosternal joint. Lateral
    edges of the manubrium connect with the first set of ribs directly and the clavicles
    at the sternoclavicular joints. The superior border features the jugular (suprasternal)
    notch, a palpable depression between the clavicular articulations. As a key structural
    element of the anterior thoracic skeleton, the manubrium provides protection
    for underlying vital structures and offers attachment sites for muscles like
    the sternocleidomastoid.
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
