  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "The cervical vertebrae comprise the uppermost segment of the vertebral
    column, located directly beneath the skull. In humans, there are typically seven
    cervical vertebrae, labeled C1 through C7. Each vertebra has distinct features:\r
    C1 (Atlas): This ring-like vertebra supports the skull and permits the \"nodding\"
    motion of the head.\r C2 (Axis): Characterized by the dens (odontoid process),
    it allows the head to rotate laterally, as in the \"shaking\" motion.\r C3-C7:
    These vertebrae are more standard in appearance but distinguishable by their
    bifid (split) spinous processes and transverse foramina \u2014 holes in the transverse
    processes for vertebral artery passage."
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
