  m_text: The xiphoid process is the smallest and most inferior segment of the sternum.
    Initially cartilaginous, it often ossifies with age and becomes more bony in
    structure. Located at the inferior end of the sternal body, this pointed structure
    serves as an attachment site for abdominal muscles, including the rectus abdominis.
    It's crucial to be aware of its location during medical procedures, such as cardiopulmonary
    resuscitation (CPR), to avoid causing injury. The xiphoid process also assists
    as an anatomical landmark in various clinical evaluations, particularly related
    to the diaphragm and liver's upper border.
