  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: The right parietal bone is one of the two parietal bones situated in the
    human skull. Located on the side and roof of the cranial cavity, it forms a large
    part of the skull's superior and lateral aspects. This broad, curved bone joins
    its counterpart, the right parietal bone, at the sagittal suture and meets the
    frontal bone anteriorly at the coronal suture. Its primary function is to provide
    protection to the underlying brain tissue and offer structural support to the
    skull. The inner surface houses grooves and channels for blood vessels and provides
    attachment sites for some of the brain's protective layers, known as the meninges.
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
