      m_Calls: []
  m_text: The acromion is a bony extension of the scapula that projects laterally
    and anteriorly over the shoulder joint. It forms the highest point of the shoulder,
    palpable as the bony tip. The acromion articulates with the lateral end of the
    clavicle, forming the acromioclavicular joint. This structure serves as an attachment
    site for various muscles and ligaments, including portions of the deltoid and
    trapezius muscles. The acromion plays a crucial role in maintaining the stability
    of the shoulder joint and is involved in several movements, including lifting
    the arm. Its shape and relation to the underlying rotator cuff tendons are vital
    in orthopedics, particularly regarding impingement syndromes.
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
