  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: 'The phalanges of the hand are the bones constituting the fingers and thumb.
    They come in three distinct types, based on their location:



    Proximal Phalanges:
    These are the first set of bones adjacent to the metacarpals. Each finger, including
    the thumb, possesses one proximal phalanx.

    Middle Phalanges: Present in
    all fingers except the thumb, these bones are sandwiched between the proximal
    and distal phalanges.

    Distal Phalanges: These terminate the fingers, located
    at the tips.

    The thumb is distinct, having only two phalanges - a proximal
    and a distal, lacking a middle phalanx.'
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
  m_sharedMaterial: {fileID: 2180264, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
