      m_Calls: []
  m_text: The thoracic vertebrae are a set of 12 vertebrae located between the cervical
    and lumbar sections of the vertebral column, labeled T1 through T12. Distinguished
    by the presence of facets for rib attachment, they form the posterior part of
    the thoracic cage. Their spinous processes are long, pointed, and slope downward.
    These vertebrae have a moderate vertebral body size and a circular vertebral
    foramen. The angle and orientation of the thoracic articular facets enable limited
    rotation but restrict flexion and extension, ensuring stability while protecting
    vital organs in the thoracic cavity.
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
