      m_Calls: []
  m_text: Lumbar vertebrae, numbering five in total (L1 to L5), are the largest and
    strongest in the vertebral column, found between the thoracic vertebrae and the
    sacrum. These vertebrae are characterized by a substantial, kidney-shaped vertebral
    body designed to bear much of the body's weight. Their spinous processes are
    short and blunt, projecting directly backward. The lumbar region permits significant
    flexion and extension movements but limits rotation. Due to the heavy loads they
    support, lumbar vertebrae are common sites for wear-and-tear injuries and conditions
    like herniated discs.
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
  m_sharedMaterial: {fileID: 2180264, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
