      m_Calls: []
  m_text: The left ulna is one of the two long bones of the forearm, positioned on
    the medial side when the palm faces forward. It runs parallel to its counterpart,
    the radius. Proximally, the ulna features a C-shaped notch, the trochlear notch,
    which articulates with the humerus, forming the elbow joint. Distally, the ulna
    narrows and ends with a bony prominence called the head, which articulates with
    the radius. The ulna serves as a stabilizing bone during forearm movements, especially
    in flexion and extension at the elbow. Its unique design also allows the radius
    to rotate around it during pronation and supination of the forearm. Due to its
    structure and proximity to the surface, the ulna can be vulnerable to injuries,
    particularly fractures resulting from trauma or falls.
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
  m_sharedMaterial: {fileID: 2180264, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
